## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses distributed tracing fundamentals, cross-service context propagation, and practical validation strategies in CNPA stacks under load.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry tracing across HTTP, PostgreSQL, Kafka, and Redis
- Context propagation via HTTP headers (W3C Trace Context), Kafka headers, and Redis commands
- Span types: HTTP server, database query, Kafka publish/consume, and Redis operations
- Sampling strategies that balance overhead and visibility
- Observability dashboards and SLIs including p95/p99 latency, error rate, and consumer lag

## Code Example

```javascript
const { NodeSDK } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-node');
const { HttpInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-http');
const { ExpressInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-express');
const { PgInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pg');
const { KafkaInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-kafkajs');
const { RedisInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-ioredis');
const { JaegerExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-jaeger');

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  serviceName: 'cnpa-service',
  instrumentations: [
    new HttpInstrumentation(),
    new ExpressInstrumentation(),
    new PgInstrumentation(),
    new KafkaInstrumentation(),
    new RedisInstrumentation(),
  ],
  traceExporter: new JaegerExporter(),
});

sdk.start();
```

This SDK setup instruments all components in the CNPA stack, automatically creating spans for HTTP requests, database queries, Kafka messages, and Redis operations with full context propagation.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical observability skills in a CNPA stack, ensuring candidates understand how to trace requests end-to-end rather than isolating individual components.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry across HTTP, database, Kafka, and Redis
- Trace propagation via W3C traceparent headers
- Sampling strategies that balance detail with performance overhead
- Backend visualization tools (Jaeger, Tempo) and troubleshooting workflows
- End-to-end verification under controlled test conditions

## Code Example

```javascript
// OpenTelemetry setup for Node.js HTTP API
const { NodeSDK } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-node');
const { HttpInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-http');
const { PgInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pg');
const { KafkaInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-kafkajs');
const { RedisInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-ioredis');
const { JaegerExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-jaeger');
const { BatchSpanProcessor } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-base');

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  serviceName: 'cnpa-api',
  spanProcessor: new BatchSpanProcessor(new JaegerExporter()),
  instrumentations: [
    new HttpInstrumentation(),
    new PgInstrumentation(),
    new KafkaInstrumentation(),
    new RedisInstrumentation(),
  ],
});

sdk.start();
```

This instrumentation automatically propagates W3C Trace Context headers across all components, ensuring end-to-end trace continuity.
